media: mediatek: vcodec: allow concurrent VCP encoder sessions

The VCP encoder admitted a single session per device, so a second encoder
client -- or any client started while another was streaming -- failed
with -EBUSY. The firmware protocol already supports several instances:
VCP_ENC_INIT returns a per-instance id, the engine keeps an instance
list, and the VENC block has two cores. Only the AP-side bookkeeping was
single-owner.

- remoteproc: turn mtk_vcp_claim()/release() into a reference count so
  the encoder and decoder backends can share one booted VCP. Each
  rproc_boot is balanced by one rproc_shutdown; the first claim records
  the owner for diagnostics and the last release clears it.

- encoder: replace the global vcp_session pointer with a per-device list
  of handles and give every open its own mtk_vcp_venc instance. The
  completion worker drains all live sessions, and stop() frees its own
  instance without waiting for the whole VCP to go idle. Quarantine and
  recovery now walk the list instead of one saved handle.

- vcodec/vcp: aggregate the shared VCORE DVFS request across instances
  (the rail follows the highest outstanding request, recomputed on
  release) instead of one owner, and drop the POWER_ON single-owner gate.

Tested on xaga: two and three concurrent encoder processes at 1280x720
complete without HW_BUSY and produce stream-valid H.264.

Source update: 42fd38c1300f41a4052a80ebabb4cac148322bc0
author Hecheng Yu <kde-yyds@qq.com> 1791133489 +0800
Link: https://github.com/MT6895-Mainline/linux/commit/42fd38c1300f41a4052a80ebabb4cac148322bc0

media: mediatek: vcp: flush a held access unit when a drain arrives

A drain (V4L2_DEC_CMD_STOP) that arrives while the firmware still holds an
access unit could never complete: the normal drain path runs only when no
source buffer is queued, and the firmware will not release a reorder-held
unit without a flush. The client then waits forever for the last frames,
which is what froze playback at end of stream and after a seek.

Flush the firmware from the source wait when a drain is pending, give it a
moment to release the bitstream, then hand the buffer back through the
shared release path.

Source update: 75716ec18e16409fc8885db1e8a9d73ca2dca1c9
author Hecheng Yu <kde-yyds@qq.com> 1791144258 +0800
Link: https://github.com/MT6895-Mainline/linux/commit/75716ec18e16409fc8885db1e8a9d73ca2dca1c9

media: mediatek: vcodec: allow concurrent VCP decoder sessions

The stateful VCP decoder accepted one session at a time: the frontend kept a
single vdec_ctx, and each session registered the one MTK_VCP_DECODER IPI
handler, so a second mtk_vcp_ipc_register failed with -EBUSY.

Follow the vendor model instead: one decoder engine registers the handler
once and routes each message to the session whose cookie matches the
ap_inst_addr the firmware echoes, and the frontend tracks contexts in a list.
Every open still gets its own mtk_vcp_vdec instance and firmware session, so
several decoders run at once. hw_stop()/quiesce now run only when the last
session leaves the VCP.

session_claim() is re-entrant: the bridge re-claims the same context on
STOP/START, so neither the list add nor the VCP reference may repeat. Without
that, the second claim corrupted the context list and leaked the boot count,
which wedged every later open.

Verified on xaga at 720p30 H.264 with B-frames: sequential, two and three
concurrent decodes all complete with full output; the checked frames match a
software decode bit-exactly (PSNR inf).

Source update: 0ec065a60dc8909205cab3d31df6797d89aa44a2
author Hecheng Yu <kde-yyds@qq.com> 1791173503 +0800
Link: https://github.com/MT6895-Mainline/linux/commit/0ec065a60dc8909205cab3d31df6797d89aa44a2

media: mediatek: vcodec: size the VDEC OPP from a declared decode rate

The decoder's OPP request is W*H*fps matched against the frequency
ladder, but fps was a hardcoded 60 (75 for HEVC/MT2T) standing in for one
panel refresh. Everything at or below 218.4M request units therefore
landed on the lowest step: 720p, 1080p and 2460x1080 all ran at 249.6 MHz
no matter what the client was doing with them.

Add V4L2_CID_MPEG_MTK_DECODE_OPERATION_RATE (MTK_BASE+16, the control the
downstream decoder carries a client's declared rate in) and let it raise
the frame rate the request is sized for. Measured on xaga at 1080p: 249.6
MHz and 106 fps at the floor, 416 MHz and 147 fps with the rate declared,
and the raised-clock runs are far more repeatable (146.9-147.6 fps against
100-119). A 4K stream declaring more than the table holds saturates at 660
MHz instead of failing every frame, so the hint is clamped to the top OPP
rather than rejected.

The declared rate is a floor on the request, not an override of it:
declaring less than the codec floor does not lower the step, because the
pixel rate is a provisioning floor rather than a measurement of the stream
and only the raising direction has evidence behind it. A client that wants
the decoder clocked down is a separate measurement.

The rate cannot be derived anywhere in the stack - no VAAPI decode
structure carries a frame rate, the bridge owns the OUTPUT timestamp so no
PTS reaches the driver, and the VUI is not in the buffers ffmpeg hands over
- so it arrives as an explicit hint through the control.

Note this does not make the decoder fast. Holding the resolution fixed and
moving only the OPP shows a ~3.16 ms per-frame floor that no step lifts: a
256x144 stream decodes at 317 fps at 249.6 MHz and at 317 fps at 416 MHz,
and h264_v4l2m2m does the same tiny stream at 2.59 ms per frame, so ~2.6 ms
is one synchronous kernel and VCP firmware round trip per frame. Above that
floor the per-pixel cost does scale with the clock, which is where the 39%
comes from.

Signed-off-by: Hecheng Yu <kde-yyds@qq.com>

Source update: c20cf8274ee90b786d11ee9f6af9970d2d0ab374
author Hecheng Yu <kde-yyds@qq.com> 1791282798 +0800
Link: https://github.com/MT6895-Mainline/linux/commit/c20cf8274ee90b786d11ee9f6af9970d2d0ab374

media: mediatek: vcodec: let the VCP decoder's CAPTURE queue exceed 32 buffers

vb2 caps a queue at VB2_MAX_FRAME (32) unless the driver raises
q->max_num_buffers, and this frontend left it at the default. The VAAPI
bridge sizes its CAPTURE pool to the client's surface count plus a reserve,
so the pool reached 32 exactly when VLC's own pool did and CREATE_BUFS past
that failed:

  grow CAPTURE: CREATE_BUFS +13 (have 32) failed

A seek that arrived while the client held every buffer then left the
firmware with nowhere to deliver the reorder tail, and it stopped retiring
access units altogether: the VCP goes completely silent - no FREE_BITSTREAM,
no FREE_FRAME, no mailbox traffic - for 15 to 30 s, the bridge's OUTPUT ring
never refills, and the session dies with VA_STATUS_ERROR_DECODING_ERROR.
Measured over 24 seeks with the pool stuck at 32: 17 stalls, 3520 decode
errors, 1678 frames, one dead session. With the pool able to hold surfaces
plus a reserve the same run has none of those and decodes 5797 frames.

The per-queue limit is what the core allocates bufs[] and bufs_bitmap from,
and vb2_core_reqbufs()/vb2_core_create_bufs() clamp to it, so raising it
here is all that is needed. VB2_MAX_FRAME and VIDEO_MAX_FRAME are UAPI and
stay at 32.

Signed-off-by: Hecheng Yu <kde-yyds@qq.com>

Source update: 3ab4554daaed6849c46f2f9ffd1f344ca1689c05
author Hecheng Yu <kde-yyds@qq.com> 1791296840 +0800
Link: https://github.com/MT6895-Mainline/linux/commit/3ab4554daaed6849c46f2f9ffd1f344ca1689c05

media: mediatek: vcodec: default the VDEC OPP from a resolution op-rate table

The decoder's OPP request falls back to a flat codec floor when the client
declares no operation rate: 60 for H.264 and 75 for HEVC/MT2T. Everything at
or below 1080p therefore lands on the 218.4 MHz step, and the control that
can raise it - V4L2_CID_MPEG_MTK_DECODE_OPERATION_RATE - is only consulted
when a client sets it, which nothing does by default. Ordinary playback is
clock-starved: measured through the VAAPI bridge at 249.6 MHz, 109 fps at
1080p and 81 fps at 2460x1080.

The vendor decoder does not depend on the client declaring anything: its
find_dflt_op_rate() looks the codec and the decoded pixel count up in the DT
max-op-rate-table and provisions from that. Do the same here, with the 120 fps
tier widened to cover the panel's 2560x1600 and 3200x2000 modes, which the
vendor table leaves at 60 because it buckets them with 4K:

  1920x1088 -> 180 -> 416 MHz   bridge 109 -> 122 fps
  2464x1088 -> 120 -> 416 MHz   92 -> 112 (H.264), 81 -> 103 (HEVC)
  2560x1600 -> 120 -> 594 MHz   71 ->  98 (H.264), 77 ->  95 (HEVC)
  3200x2000 -> 120 -> 660 MHz   67 ->  77 (H.264); 69 -> 71 (HEVC), the
                                request is clamped to 103 fps by the top step
  3840x2160 ->  60 -> 594 MHz   unchanged - the flat floor already lands here

The table is consulted only while the operation-rate control is unset, so a
client that declares a rate still wins, and vdec_codec_floor_fps() stays
underneath so the table can only raise the request. The 4K rows are no-ops
against the old floor and are kept only so the ladder is complete; 2160p HEVC
still reaches 660 MHz from its 75 fps floor. The top-step clamp is unchanged,
so a geometry the rail cannot serve fails exactly as it did before.

The OPP log line now names its source, so a session that was provisioned by
the table is distinguishable from one a client asked for:

  default rate 120 -> 103 fps for the 3200x2000 OPP request (clamped ...)
  operation rate 240 -> 161 fps for the 2560x1600 OPP request (clamped ...)

Signed-off-by: Hecheng Yu <kde-yyds@qq.com>

Source update: 25418005f50b108969d2f65f671fd71c021ee8ad
author Hecheng Yu <kde-yyds@qq.com> 1791361244 +0800
Link: https://github.com/MT6895-Mainline/linux/commit/25418005f50b108969d2f65f671fd71c021ee8ad

media: mediatek: vcodec: decode straight into the client's CAPTURE buffer

The VCP firmware only writes tiled pictures, and downstream hands it the
client's own imported dma-buf to write into with no kernel detile. Do the
same: when CAPTURE is negotiated as MM21/MT2T, the decoder's reference
frames are the client's buffers, so nothing is copied between what the
firmware writes and what the client reads.

A client buffer is submitted with mtk_vcp_vdec_frame() carrying its
cookie and the firmware releases it with a FREE_FRAME event.
mtk_vcp_vdec_start() now reports the cookie the firmware assigned to the
frame buffer it registered, so the frontend can match those events to the
right surface; it used to return the IPC layer's slot index, which is a
different numbering entirely.

Only pool_count - 4 of the client's buffers are absorbed, so some are
always left in the driver's queue - one has to carry the LAST marker when
the stream drains, or the client blocks waiting for the end of a stream
the driver never signals. The existing guard only required dpb + 3
buffers in total, and with a reserve of 4 that could hand the firmware
fewer surfaces than its own DPB, which it answers by timing out and
taking the VCP down. Trade the reserve down to two slots when that is
what it takes to keep the firmware fed.

This layout only works for a client that keeps its whole DPB queued: one
that hands frames on does, one that reads them back does not. Which
layout a session uses is the capture format the client negotiates, so the
bridge decides that per client.

Source update: f1c0c8245d163fece46699612b8322d11e26348d
author Hecheng Yu <kde-yyds@qq.com> 1793614359 +0800
Link: https://github.com/MT6895-Mainline/linux/commit/f1c0c8245d163fece46699612b8322d11e26348d

media: mediatek: vcodec: make the VCP decoder usable from a C2 client

The stateful decoder was written for the VA-API bridge, which always knows the
picture geometry up front and hands the firmware a frame buffer with every
access unit. Android's Codec2 stack does neither, and the stream stalled in
four separate ways. Each fix is independent of the client:

 - Poll the firmware-owned rings. In INPUT_DRIVEN_PUT_FRM mode the firmware
   reports finished pictures in vsi->display and released bitstreams in
   vsi->free_bs without raising an IPI, so a client that only waits for
   interrupts never sees a frame. mtk_vcp_vdec_collect() drains both rings and
   collect_events() calls it before walking the event list.

 - Handle configuration-only access units. Codec2 delivers the codec
   configuration as its own buffers (SPS and PPS one each, a 2.4 KB packet for
   HEVC), and the firmware either rejects them or holds the bitstream forever.
   au_has_slice() detects them, the driver consumes the buffer and replays the
   parameter sets in front of the first access unit that carries a slice, which
   is what the firmware needs to keep a sequence header across the capture
   renegotiation.

 - Pair a frame buffer with the header pass. HEVC reports input_driven = 0 and
   answers AP_START with -1 when the START names no frame, which is exactly the
   situation for the pass that only parses the sequence header: the client has
   no picture-sized buffers queued yet. probe_surface() allocates one of the
   CAPTURE geometry and reuses it until the client takes over.

 - Do not force a second capture restart. The header pass already publishes
   SOURCE_CHANGE, so a client that renegotiated CAPTURE before the decode pass
   has nothing left to restart; waiting for it stalled the stream, and treating
   the geometry that every rebuilt firmware session re-announces as a new
   resolution change made the driver rebuild the session for every frame.
   capture_matches_picture() and the primed flag tell the two apart.

With these, a Codec2 client decodes H.264, HEVC, VP9 and VP8 bit-exactly on the
VCP firmware, and the VA-API bridge on the same kernel is unchanged.
